+Near ties in Staffer routes. The cheapest route wins, and costs equal to 12 significant digits are a tie. A route ties for the cheapest when its exact cost, the real sum of its float hop and terminal costs with no rounding, is within one part in 10^12 (relative 1e-12) of the cheapest route's exact cost. Among tied routes the one with the fewest steps wins, then the one with the lower exact cost, and any remaining tie is broken the same way every time (the smallest person ids, read from the destination end). The reported cost is still the route's hop and terminal costs added as floats in order from the origin. Before this rule, which of two routes that cost the same on paper won depended on how the floating-point sums happened to round, and two searches could pick different routes. Alternative routes under `alternative_strategy=ranked` follow the same rule: the next alternative accepted is, among the pending candidates whose exact cost is within one part in 10^12 of the cheapest pending one, the one with the fewest steps, then the one with the lower exact cost, then the one whose person ids read from the destination end are smallest. Two alternatives inside that width can therefore be listed in an order that differs from their reported costs by rounding; alternatives further apart than that are ranked by cost as before. `distinct` and `independent` choose each route with one such search.
 
